  "content": {
    "id": "p148-parity-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Determine whether the following are even, odd or neither.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p148-parity-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=x^2+x$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Neither; $f(-x)=x^2-x$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=(-x)^2+(-x)\\\\f(-x)&=x^2-x.\\end{align*}$$\nNeither $f(-x)=f(x)$ nor $f(-x)=-f(x)$ holds for every real $x$. Therefore the function is neither even nor odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p148-parity-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=x^3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Odd; $f(-x)=-x^3$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=(-x)^3\\\\f(-x)&=-x^3\\\\f(-x)&=-f(x).\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore the function is odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p148-parity-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=x-x^3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Odd; $f(-x)=-x+x^3$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=(-x)-(-x)^3\\\\f(-x)&=-x+x^3\\\\f(-x)&=-(x-x^3)\\\\f(-x)&=-f(x).\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore the function is odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p148-parity-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Odd; $f(-x)=-\\frac{1}{x}$.",
          "worked": "The domain $x\\ne0$ is symmetric about $0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=\\frac{1}{-x}\\\\f(-x)&=-\\frac{1}{x}\\\\f(-x)&=-f(x).\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore the function is odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      },
      {
        "id": "p148-parity-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=2^x$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Neither; $f(-x)=2^{-x}$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=2^{-x}\\\\f(-1)&=\\frac{1}{2}\\\\f(1)&=2.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $\\frac{1}{2}\\ne2$ and $\\frac{1}{2}\\ne-2$, neither defining identity holds for every $x$. Therefore the function is neither even nor odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      },
      {
        "id": "p148-parity-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{1-x^2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Even; $f(-x)=\\sqrt{1-x^2}$.",
          "worked": "The domain $[-1,1]$ is symmetric about $0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=\\sqrt{1-(-x)^2}\\\\f(-x)&=\\sqrt{1-x^2}\\\\f(-x)&=f(x).\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore the function is even.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p148-parity-g",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=x+\\frac{1}{x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Odd; $f(-x)=-x-\\frac{1}{x}$.",
          "worked": "The domain $x\\ne0$ is symmetric about $0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=-x+\\frac{1}{-x}\\\\f(-x)&=-x-\\frac{1}{x}\\\\f(-x)&=-\\left(x+\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\\\f(-x)&=-f(x).\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore the function is odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      },
      {
        "id": "p148-parity-h",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "h",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{x+1}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Neither; $f(-x)=-\\frac{1+x}{1-x}$.",
          "worked": "The domain is $\\mathbb{R}\\setminus\\{-1\\}$. For $x\\ne1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=\\frac{-x-1}{-x+1}\\\\f(-x)&=-\\frac{1+x}{1-x}.\\end{align*}$$\nNeither parity identity holds throughout the original domain. In particular, $1$ is in the domain but $-1$ is not, so $f(-x)$ is undefined at $x=1$. Therefore the function is neither even nor odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
  },
